}
```

## 🔗 Integration with Your Product API

### **API Architecture Pattern**
```
Store Locator Frontend
  ├── GET /api/stores → Returns store locations + basic info
  ├── GET /api/inventory?storeId=&productIds= → Returns live inventory
  ├── WebSocket /inventory-updates → Real-time stock changes
  └── POST /api/reservations → Reserve products for in-store pickup
```

### **Inventory Data Flow**
1. **User searches for a product** → Frontend calls your product API
2. **API returns product availability** per store location
3. **Map filters stores** to only show those with inventory
4. **Real-time updates** via WebSocket when inventory changes
5. **Reservation system** holds items for pickup

### **Sample Product API Response**
```json
{
  "productId": "prod_123",
  "stores": [
    {
      "storeId": "store_001",
      "quantity": 5,
      "location": { "lat": 40.7128, "lng": -74.0060 },
      "lastUpdated": "2024-01-15T10:30:00Z"
    }
  ]
}
```

## 💰 Cost-Effective Implementation

### **Use Essentials SKUs**
- **Maps JavaScript API** (Dynamic Maps) - $7 per 1,000 loads
- **Places UI Kit** - More cost-effective than Places API
- **Distance Matrix API** - $5 per 1,000 elements

### **Optimization Strategies**
1. **Cache store locations** client-side (refresh every 24h)
2. **Batch inventory requests** for multiple stores
3. **Use Places UI Kit** instead of custom Places API implementation
4. **Implement smart loading** - only load inventory for visible stores
5. **Set up quota alerts** in Google Cloud Console

## ✅ Production Readiness Checklist

- [ ] **API Key Security**: Restrict by HTTP referrer, IP if possible
- [ ] **Error Handling**: Network failures, quota limits, inventory API errors
- [ ] **Loading States**: Skeleton screens for map and inventory
- [ ] **Accessibility**: ARIA labels, keyboard navigation, screen reader support
- [ ] **Mobile Responsive**: Touch-friendly markers, responsive layout
- [ ] **Analytics**: Track store searches, directions requests, inventory checks
- [ ] **Terms Compliance**: Display "Powered by Google" attribution
- [ ] **Performance**: Lazy load map, optimize images, minimize API calls
- [ ] **Testing**: Cross-browser, mobile devices, slow network conditions

## 📚 Essential Resources

1. **Product Locator Implementation Guide**: [developers.google.com/maps/solutions/product-locator/best-practices](https://developers.google.com/maps/solutions/product-locator/best-practices?utm_source=gmp-code-assist)
2. **Store Locator Codelab**: [Simple Store Locator](https://developers.google.com/codelabs/maps-platform/google-maps-simple-store-locator?utm_source=gmp-code-assist)
3. **Places UI Kit Documentation**: [developers.google.com/maps/documentation/javascript/places-ui-kit](https://developers.google.com/maps/documentation/javascript/places-ui-kit/overview?utm_source=gmp-code-assist)
4. **Extended Component Library**: [Store Locator Web Component](https://github.com/googlemaps/extended-component-library?utm_source=gmp-code-assist)

## 🚨 Important Compliance Notes

- **Attribution**: Always display "Powered by Google" on your map
- **API Terms**: Review [Google Maps Platform Terms of Service](https://cloud.google.com/maps-platform/terms?utm_source=gmp-code-assist)
- **EEA Compliance**: If your users are in the European Economic Area, ensure compliance with EEA Terms

**Next Steps**: Start with the [Quick Builder Locator Plus solution](https://console.cloud.google.com/google/maps-apis/buildready?utm_source=gmp-code-assist) to prototype in minutes, then integrate your product API for live inventory data. The key is to build incrementally: start with basic store locations, add inventory filtering, then enhance with real-time updates and advanced features.

