### Handle JWT Token Expired Error

Source: https://context7.com/auth0/node-jsonwebtoken/llms.txt

Demonstrates how to catch and handle a `TokenExpiredError` when verifying a JWT. This occurs when the token's expiration time has passed. The example shows how to access `expiredAt`, `name`, and `message` properties of the error.

```javascript
const jwt = require('jsonwebtoken');

// Handle TokenExpiredError
const expiredToken = jwt.sign(
  { userId: 12345 },
  'secret',
  { expiresIn: '1s' }
);

setTimeout(() => {
  jwt.verify(expiredToken, 'secret', (err, decoded) => {
    if (err) {
      if (err instanceof jwt.TokenExpiredError) {
        console.error('Token expired at:', err.expiredAt);
        console.error('Error name:', err.name); // 'TokenExpiredError'
        console.error('Error message:', err.message); // 'jwt expired'
        // Typical response: prompt user to refresh token or re-authenticate
      }
    }
  });
}, 2000);
```

--------------------------------

### Verify JWT with Clock Tolerance

Source: https://context7.com/auth0/node-jsonwebtoken/llms.txt

This example shows how to verify a JWT while allowing for a specified clock tolerance, which is useful for distributed systems where server clocks may not be perfectly synchronized. The 'jsonwebtoken' library is used.

```javascript
const clockSkewToken = jwt.sign(
  { userId: 12345 },
  'secret',
  { expiresIn: '1h' }
);

// Verify with clock tolerance (useful for distributed systems)
jwt.verify(clockSkewToken, 'secret', {
  clockTolerance: 30 // allow 30 seconds difference
}, (err, decoded) => {
  console.log('Verified with clock tolerance:', decoded);
});
```

--------------------------------

### Restrict Allowed Algorithms During JWT Verification (JavaScript)

Source: https://context7.com/auth0/node-jsonwebtoken/llms.txt

Demonstrates how to enhance security by explicitly specifying allowed algorithms during JWT verification using the `algorithms` option. This prevents attackers from using weaker or unintended algorithms, even if they obtain a token signed with a different one.

```javascript
const jwt = require('jsonwebtoken');

// Algorithm restriction during verification (recommended for security)
try {
  jwt.verify(hmacToken256, rsaPublicKey, { algorithms: ['RS256'] });
} catch (err) {
  console.error('Algorithm mismatch detected:', err.message); // 'invalid signature'
}

// Allow multiple algorithms
const multiAlgDecoded = jwt.verify(token, key, {
  algorithms: ['RS256', 'RS384', 'RS512', 'ES256']
});
```", annotations=None, meta=None)]

